{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And to the sons of Abraham's concubines</b> — On the level of plain sense: because it was said to him (Genesis 21:12), \"for in Isaac shall seed be called to you,\" not in another seed, all his women were to him concubines; they were not reckoned as wives, since their seed is not among his heirs. For Hagar, Sarah's maidservant, was his concubine, but Keturah he took as a wife. And if she were a maidservant in his household and he took her as a concubine, Scripture would not have said, \"and he took a wife and her name was Keturah\"; rather, she would be called a concubine in the verse, for the reason I explained. And in Chronicles (1 Chronicles 1:32) it is written, \"and the sons of Keturah, Abraham's concubine.\" Now, Abraham took a wife from the daughters of Canaan, and if you should say she was Egyptian or from the land of the Philistines — behold, he did not send to his land and to his birthplace as he did with his son, for he was preserving only the seed of Isaac, since upon him the covenant was sealed. And furthermore, Scripture did not say, \"and he took a wife, Keturah daughter of so-and-so the Hivite or the Philistine or the Egyptian, from such-and-such a land,\" as it said regarding Esau's wives (Genesis 26:34) and others; rather, it mentioned her name alone, for she was a Canaanite, and it abbreviated her lineage. And so Scripture does in many places when it is not particular about their lineage. And perhaps she is called a concubine because she was a maidservant from a slave family, and if she was a maidservant in his household and he came to her, he would not mention her lineage, for it is not the way of Scripture to mention regarding the Matriarchs anything but their names, as with Zilpah and Bilhah. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 25:6): \"wives — with a ketubbah; concubines — without a ketubbah,\" as it says regarding the wives and concubines of David in Sanhedrin (21a). But this is not correct, for she is not called a concubine unless she is without kiddushin, since the ketubbah is of Rabbinic decree. And the version in Sanhedrin is: \"a concubine — without ketubbah and without kiddushin.\" But it is possible that even the children of Noah, when they would marry women according to their custom through intercourse, would be accustomed to write them a Mohar and a gift; and when their intent was that she be a concubine to them and he may send her away whenever he wishes, and her children would not inherit his property together with his sons, he would not write her anything. And according to the opinion of our Sages (Bereshit Rabbah 61:4) that she is Hagar — behold, she is certainly a concubine."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:35:17.785645405Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}